# Graphical linear algebra, level 1. A vector is a BUNDLE — n bare
# wires, typed Intⁿ — and the generators are width-polymorphic: one
# word, every dimension. No boxes: the stack is the vector.
#   dupN : aⁿ ⇒ aⁿ aⁿ            copy (Δ)
#   addN : Intⁿ Intⁿ ⇒ Intⁿ     pointwise add (∇)
#   zipN : aⁿ bⁿ ⇒ Box(a b)ⁿ   pair two bundles, one wire each
#   sumN : Intⁿ ⇒ Int           fold a bundle
# A matrix is just a value Fn⟨Intⁿ ⇒ Intᵐ⟩.
#
# That is the VECTOR half of graphical linear algebra.  The MORPHISM
# half — the PROP presented as a `theory`, with copy/add/zero/discard
# as slots, no `embed`, and transposition as a law-checked exchange of
# the comonoid and the monoid — is `examples/linear.braid` (2026-09-21).

1 2 3 >> dupN >> addN >> sumN >> print      # Δ then ∇ = x+x pointwise → 12

# the bialgebra check, operationally: copy-then-add = scale-by-2
def dbl  = dupN >> addN
def dblS = 2 ... >> scaleN
20 30 >> dbl  >> sumN >> print              # 100
20 30 >> dblS >> sumN >> print              # 100

# dot product: zip the bundles, fold the boxed pairs — dimension-
# checked (zipN forces the two widths equal at compile time)
def dot = zipN >> [(acc p -> p >> unBox >> (a b -> (a b >> *) acc >> +))] 0 ... >> foldExp
1 2 3 4 5 6 >> dot >> print                 # 1·4 + 2·5 + 3·6 = 32
